        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-3c83d5dfba3137c8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Julien directly and deliberately avows as true that he has always loved Mme de Rênal and has loved only her. The retrospective perfect and the framing imperative “Sache que” strongly support T. The statement contains no independent promise or relational undertaking, and the intense emotion surrounding it does not establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The tears, kisses, and emotional extremity make the delivery highly passionate, but the text presents the words as an intelligible avowal rather than a reflex-like discharge.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Julien directly addresses Mme de Rênal in spoken dialogue: “je t'ai toujours aimée.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs during Julien and Mme de Rênal's emotionally intense prison reunion after she has forgiven him. It contrasts his enduring love for her with his relationship to Mathilde; his later claim that Mathilde is his wife only “in appearance” reinforces the exclusivity asserted here. The scene strongly supports sincerity, but sincerity and passion remain contextual features rather than separate core forces.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Sache que je t'ai toujours aimée, que je n'ai aimé que toi.”\",\"supports\":\"The imperative “Sache” presents the proposition for Mme de Rênal to accept as true, while “toujours” and “que toi” specify enduring and exclusive love; this strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Mme de Rênal is enraptured, and the pair weep silently; the narrator says Julien had never experienced such a moment.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes intense and apparently sincere emotional context, while not independently establishing expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later Julien says of Mathilde: “C'est ma femme, mais ce n'est pas ma maîtresse...” and the narrator states, “Jamais il n'avait été aussi fou d'amour.”\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates the avowal's contextual sincerity and its contrast between Mme de Rênal and Mathilde.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter XLIII and tagged as affirmative perfect-form explicit first-to-second-person love language.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical form and identification of the highlighted love utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as a direct avowal of enduring love. Emotional intensity is adequately represented contextually, without requiring E, and no commitment force or residual category is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
